@* Ported from OTS/OpeningInventory.ascx + .ascx.cs + .ascx.designer.cs - the one-time-setup
   opening-stock entry screen: a growable grid of item/store/quantity/unit/amount rows, saved in
   one go through the opening-inventory service.

   ⚠ AccessLevel.AdminOnly IS PRESERVED, and ⚠ ITransaction IS IMPLEMENTED - this is the only
   ITransaction in the module and it is easy to miss, because the class declares it with no
   OverridePath beside it. It is what makes MixERPUserControl's
   CheckTransactionPostingStatusAsync apply: the host's implementation performs the
   `control is ITransaction` test, so dropping the interface would let a user in restricted
   transaction mode post opening stock.

   ⚠ THE "ALREADY ENTERED" GUARD IS REAL LOGIC AND IS PRESERVED, including its position. The
   legacy OnControlLoad built the header and the top panel, THEN checked
   `DbConfig.GetSwitch(catalog, "AllowMultipleOpeningInventory")` and
   `Data.OpeningInventory.Exists(catalog, officeId)`, and when an entry existed and the switch
   was off it added `div.ui.positive.message` with Labels.OpeningInventoryAlreadyEntered and
   RETURNED - so the grid, the bottom panel and the Save button were never rendered. That is
   reproduced exactly: header and top panel always, then either the message OR the grid +
   bottom panel. This is what stops a second opening balance being posted, so it is not a
   cosmetic branch.
   Both reads need the catalog and the office id, which are asynchronous, so they run in
   OnInitializedAsync and the screen renders nothing below the top panel until they have
   answered (`loaded`).

   ⚠ THE GRID IS A POSITIONAL JAVASCRIPT CONTRACT - DO NOT REORDER OR DROP A COLUMN.
   js/ots/OpeningInventory.ascx.js builds every data row itself and then addRowToTable() reads
   it back by INDEX: getColumnText(row, 0)=ItemCode, 1=ItemName, 2=StoreName, 3=Quantity,
   4=UnitName, 5=Amount, 6=Total, and setColumnText writes 3, 5 and 6 when it merges a duplicate
   row. The SERVICE then parses the same shape positionally (item[0], item[2], item[3],
   item[4], item[5] - skipping 1 and 6). So all EIGHT columns are emitted in exactly the order
   CreateHeaderRow/CreateControlRow added them, headers as <th> carrying their <label for>, and
   the per-cell css classes ("integer text-right" / "currency text-right") kept verbatim -
   `tableToJSON` and the formatting helpers both depend on them.
     The header row was moved into TableRowSection.TableHeader, so it is a real <thead>; the
     control row keeps its `class="ui footer-row form"` and is the LAST row of the <tbody>,
     which is what `tbody tr:not(:last-child)` and `tr:last` in the script mean. The
     `saveButton` guard `tbody tr.length < 2` counts that control row, so the <tbody> must hold
     the control row and nothing else at first render.
     ⚠ `class=""` IS EMITTED ON THE CELLS THAT HAD NO CLASS. AddHeaderCell/AddInputTextControlCell
     called Attributes.Add("class", cssClass) unconditionally, including with "", so the
     rendered attribute was there and empty. Preserved so the markup is byte-identical.
     ⚠ The Add button's caption is the LITERAL "Add", not Titles.Add, unlike every other caption
     on this screen. Upstream localization gap, preserved as found.

   ✅ #ItemIdHidden IS NOW EMITTED - this closes the LAST of the host obligations recorded in
   Modules/migration-summary.md §6 and §10. StockAdjustmentFactory/ScrudFactory's F4 item popup
   writes the chosen item id into `#ItemIdHidden`, and the element is owned by the CONSUMER
   PAGE, not by the control. This screen's script declares
   `var itemIdHidden = $("#ItemIdHidden");` at the top - against an element the legacy markup
   never rendered.
     ⚠ WORTH KNOWING: adding it is INERT here, which is why it is safe. `itemIdHidden` is
     declared and then NEVER USED again anywhere in that file (unlike Inventory's Entry screens,
     where the popup genuinely writes through it), so the element switches nothing on; it
     satisfies the documented contract without newly activating a lookup. The same cannot be
     said of #StoreIdHidden, #UnitIdHidden, #UnitNameHidden and #OpeningInventoryGridViewHidden,
     which the script also declares and which have NEVER existed either - and two of those ARE
     used (loadUnits() reads #UnitIdHidden for the pre-selected unit, and unitSelect.change()
     writes both unit hidden fields). They are deliberately NOT added: doing so would newly make
     the unit selection sticky across rows, which is a behaviour change. Raised separately.

   The inline <style> block (the seven #OpeningInventoryGridView column widths) stays a <style>
   element - Blazor strips <script>, not <style>.

   The controller stays the owner of every interaction: the Add button, the Save button, the
   item/store/unit ajax binds, the keyboard shortcuts and the total calculation are all its
   click/blur/change handlers reading the DOM, so none of these elements becomes @@bind or
   @@onclick. The script is loaded AFTER the first render because its top-level block reads
   #OpeningInventoryGridView and the selects out of the DOM.

   The login-derived values come from ICurrentLogin: AppUsers.GetCurrentUserDB() and
   AppUsers.GetCurrent().View.OfficeId feed the switch read, the "already entered" check and the
   DateTextBox's fiscal-calendar lookup, and all of them return an EMPTY login inside a circuit.

   The #region IDisposable of the original disposed the one DateTextBox it held as a field; the
   control is a component now and the framework owns its lifetime, so there is nothing to
   dispose.

   The original declared no OverridePath, MasterPageId, RemoveTheme or IsLandingPage override. *@

@inherits MixERP.Net.Framework.Controls.MixERPUserControl
@implements MixERP.Net.Entities.Contracts.ITransaction
@using MixERP.Net.Common.Helpers
@using MixERP.Net.Core.Modules.BackOffice.Internal
@using MixERP.Net.Entities
@using MixERP.Net.WebControls.Common
@using MixERP.Net.i18n.Resources
@inject IJSRuntime JS

<style type="text/css">
    table input[type=radio] {
        margin: 4px;
    }

    #OpeningInventoryGridView th:nth-child(1) {
        width: 10%;
    }

    #OpeningInventoryGridView th:nth-child(2) {
        width: 20%;
    }

    #OpeningInventoryGridView th:nth-child(3) {
        width: 15%;
    }

    #OpeningInventoryGridView th:nth-child(4) {
        width: 15%;
    }

    #OpeningInventoryGridView th:nth-child(5) {
        width: 15%;
    }

    #OpeningInventoryGridView th:nth-child(6) {
        width: 15%;
    }

    #OpeningInventoryGridView th:nth-child(7) {
        width: 10%;
    }
</style>

@* CreateHeader(Placeholder1) *@
<h2>@Titles.OpeningInventory</h2>

@* CreateTopPanel(Placeholder1) *@
<div class="ui form">
    <div class="inline fields">
        <div class="field">
            <label for="ValueDateTextBox">@Titles.ValueDate</label>
            <DateTextBox ID="ValueDateTextBox"
                         Mode="FrequencyType.Today"
                         Catalog="@this.catalog"
                         OfficeId="@this.officeId" />
        </div>
        <div class="field">
            <label for="ReferenceNumberInputText">@Titles.ReferenceNumber</label>
            <input type="text" id="ReferenceNumberInputText" maxlength="24" />
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

@* ✅ The host-supplied hidden element the item popup's AssociatedControlId names. See the
   header: it is declared by the controller and is inert, so emitting it closes the documented
   contract without switching any behaviour on. *@
<input type="hidden" id="ItemIdHidden" />

@if (this.loaded)
{
    @if (this.alreadyEntered)
    {
        @* CreateMessage(Placeholder1) - and the original RETURNED here, so nothing below it
           was rendered. *@
        <div class="ui positive message">@Labels.OpeningInventoryAlreadyEntered</div>
    }
    else
    {
        @* CreateGridView(Placeholder1) *@
        <table class="ui table" id="OpeningInventoryGridView">
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th class=""><label for="ItemCodeInputText">@Titles.ItemCode</label></th>
                    <th class=""><label for="ItemSelect">@Titles.ItemName</label></th>
                    <th class=""><label for="StoreSelect">@Titles.StoreName</label></th>
                    <th class="integer text-right"><label for="QuantityInputText">@Titles.Quantity</label></th>
                    <th class=""><label for="UnitSelect">@Titles.Unit</label></th>
                    <th class="currency text-right"><label for="AmountInputText">@Titles.Amount</label></th>
                    <th class="currency text-right"><label for="TotalInputText">@Titles.Total</label></th>
                    <th class=""><label for="AddRowButton">@Titles.Action</label></th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr class="ui footer-row form">
                    <td><input type="text" id="ItemCodeInputText" class="" /></td>
                    <td><select id="ItemSelect"></select></td>
                    <td><select id="StoreSelect"></select></td>
                    <td><input type="text" id="QuantityInputText" class="integer text-right" /></td>
                    <td><select id="UnitSelect"></select></td>
                    <td><input type="text" id="AmountInputText" class="currency text-right" /></td>
                    <td><input type="text" id="TotalInputText" class="currency text-right" readonly="readonly" /></td>
                    @* ⚠ The caption is the literal "Add", not Titles.Add - preserved as found. *@
                    <td><input type="button" id="AddRowButton" class="small ui blue button" value="Add" /></td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        @* CreateBottomPanel(Placeholder1). NOTE: the original started from
           HtmlControlHelper.GetInlineFields() and then OVERWROTE its class with "ui form" and
           added the width, so the rendered element is a div.ui.form - not inline fields. *@
        <div class="ui form" style="width:290px;">
            <div class="field">
                <label for="StatementReferenceTextArea">@Titles.StatementReference</label>
                <textarea id="StatementReferenceTextArea" rows="4"></textarea>
            </div>
            <input type="button" id="SaveButton" class="small ui button red" value="@Titles.Save" />
        </div>
    }
}

@code {
    private const string ControllerScript =
        "_content/MixERP.Net.Core.Modules.BackOffice/js/ots/OpeningInventory.ascx.js";

    /// <summary>
    ///     The legacy <c>public override AccessLevel AccessLevel</c>, carried across unchanged.
    ///     <para>
    ///         <c>MixERPUserControlBase.AccessLevel</c> is an ABSTRACT GET-ONLY property, so it is
    ///         overridden rather than assigned - which is also what the probe needs:
    ///         <c>ModuleHost.razor</c> reads the surface off an <c>Activator.CreateInstance</c>
    ///         instance on which no lifecycle method runs, and a getter answers there.
    ///     </para>
    /// </summary>
    public override AccessLevel AccessLevel
    {
        get { return AccessLevel.AdminOnly; }
    }

    private string catalog;
    private int officeId;
    private bool loaded;
    private bool alreadyEntered;
    private bool scriptLoaded;

    /// <summary>
    ///     MixERPUserControlBase.OnControlLoad is abstract, so every ported control declares it.
    ///     The legacy body called the five Create* builders - all markup now - and performed the
    ///     "already entered" check between them; that check needs the catalog and the office id,
    ///     which are asynchronous, so it runs in OnInitializedAsync. Nothing was dropped.
    /// </summary>
    public override void OnControlLoad(object sender, EventArgs e)
    {
    }

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        //base.OnInitializedAsync runs BOTH authorization checks - never skip it, and here it is
        //also what applies CheckTransactionPostingStatusAsync to this ITransaction control.
        await base.OnInitializedAsync();

        MixERP.Net.Framework.Security.LoginInfo login = await this.CurrentLogin.GetLoginAsync();

        this.catalog = await this.CurrentLogin.GetCatalogAsync();
        this.officeId = login.OfficeId;

        //Was:
        //  bool allowMultipleOpeningInventory =
        //      DbConfig.GetSwitch(AppUsers.GetCurrentUserDB(), "AllowMultipleOpeningInventory");
        //  bool entryExists = Data.OpeningInventory.Exists(AppUsers.GetCurrentUserDB(),
        //      AppUsers.GetCurrent().View.OfficeId.ToInt());
        //  if (entryExists && !allowMultipleOpeningInventory) { CreateMessage(...); return; }
        bool allowMultipleOpeningInventory = DbConfig.GetSwitch(this.catalog, "AllowMultipleOpeningInventory");
        bool entryExists = Data.OpeningInventory.Exists(this.catalog, this.officeId);

        this.alreadyEntered = entryExists && !allowMultipleOpeningInventory;
        this.loaded = true;
    }

    /// <summary>
    ///     The controller is loaded on the render AFTER the asynchronous check has answered,
    ///     never on <c>firstRender</c>: its top-level block reads <c>#OpeningInventoryGridView</c>
    ///     and the three selects out of the DOM, and on the first render the grid has not been
    ///     decided on yet. It is also not loaded at all when the "already entered" message is
    ///     showing, because there is then no grid, no selects and no Save button for it to bind
    ///     to - which matches the original, where the elements it binds to were never rendered
    ///     either.
    /// </summary>
    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        await base.OnAfterRenderAsync(firstRender);

        if (this.scriptLoaded || !this.loaded || this.alreadyEntered)
        {
            return;
        }

        this.scriptLoaded = true;
        await ModuleScripts.LoadAsync(this.JS, ControllerScript);
    }
}
